              <h2>Why Some Apps Trigger Tantrums (And How Low-Stimulation Apps Prevent Them)</h2>
              <p>
                Have you ever noticed that your child can read a picture book for 30 minutes and happily walk away, yet
                15 minutes on a typical mobile game ends in tears when you ask to turn it off?
              </p>
              <p>
                The culprit usually isn't "the screen" itself; it's <strong>persuasive game design</strong>: rapid scene
                cuts, flashing confetti, countdown timers, streak counters, and intermittent reward loops that flood a
                developing brain with dopamine and cortisol.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Low-stimulation kids' apps</strong> are built differently:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Natural Stopping Points:</strong> Because there are no streaks, timers, or "just one more
                  level" hooks, children can pause and put the device down peacefully.</li>
                <li><strong>Acoustic &amp; Visual Calm:</strong> Soft illustrations, natural ambient sounds (rainforest
                  wind, ocean waves, acoustic instruments), and unhurried animations.</li>
                <li><strong>Co-Viewing Friendly:</strong> Aligned with American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP)
                  recommendations, calm apps leave room for parents and children to talk and wonder together.</li>
              </ul>

